1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.502
Now that
you understand your agent example,

2
00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:05.505
the next step is to add
observability to your agent code.

3
00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.742
This will give you insight
into the trajectory or sequence of steps

4
00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:11.711
your agent takes to respond
to a user's query.

5
00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.279
You'll learn how to instrument your agent,

6
00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:16.182
which means adding code
to track your agent steps,

7
00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.786
and then visualize the collected
information or traces in the Phoenix UI.

8
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.522
All right. Let's have some fun.

9
00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.124
When it comes to observing your agent,

10
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:26.693
there are a few key concepts
that are important to know.

11
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.595
The first is observability.

12
00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:31.131
Observability is a general software
concept

13
00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:35.502
that refers to having complete visibility
into every layer of your application.

14
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:40.040
In the context of LLM applications, that's
often tracking things like the prompt,

15
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.043
the response, token usage,
and then any calls

16
00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:46.046
that happen around the calls
that you're making to LLMs.

17
00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:50.083
Observability
is typically made up of traces and spans.

18
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.753
Traces refer to full run
through of your application,

19
00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:56.756
so a single trace would be one and end

20
00:00:56.756 --> 00:01:00.193
run of your application from an input
all the way through to an output.

21
00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:04.030
And then traces are comprised
of multiple spans.

22
00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:07.167
Spans are individual steps of calls

23
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:11.237
to LLMs or code, or lookups to databases
or whatever might be.

24
00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:15.008
So one trace is made of multiple spans,
and they're typically presented

25
00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:18.378
in this hierarchical way where spans
can be nested underneath each other

26
00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:21.681
to show that they're operating
within another span.

27
00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.818
And then a set of spans
will make up one trace hierarchy.

28
00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.855
Spans are typically presented
in this hierarchical manner here

29
00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:31.925
where you have individual spans
that are nested

30
00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.928
within each other,
all making up one trace.

31
00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:38.465
And you might have spans for LLM calls
as you see in the image here.

32
00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:40.200
You might have spans for tools.

33
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.501
You might have chain spans

34
00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:44.537
which are just general logic steps
that happen within a trace.

35
00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:48.408
Traces and spans come from a framework
called OpenTelemetry.

36
00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.311
OpenTelemetry is often
abbreviated to OTEL,

37
00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:54.214
and it's one of the most widely used
standards for application

38
00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:57.750
observability,
even outside of LLMs and AI agents.

39
00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.554
OTEL includes the idea of traces and spans
that are captured within your

40
00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:05.058
application, as well as some standards
for how to capture those traces and spans.

41
00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:06.259
Often this process

42
00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.262
is referred to as instrumentation,
which you'll learn more about in a second.

43
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:14.467
And then OpenTelemetry also includes
the concept of collectors and processors

44
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:19.105
to receive those traces and spans,
and then enumerate them in a platform

45
00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:22.876
that allows you to visualize those traces
and run later evaluation on them.

46
00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:25.044
Throughout this course,
you're going to use a tool

47
00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:28.248
called Arize Phoenix,
which serves as one of those collectors

48
00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:31.351
to allow you to receive,
visualize, and evaluate traces.

49
00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.687
This is
what a trace looks like in Phoenix.

50
00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.589
You're going to see a lot more of this
throughout the notebooks

51
00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.858
as you start
implementing some of these techniques.

52
00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:41.995
But you'll notice
there's one trace here of Agent Run

53
00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.964
in the image
that's made up of multiple spans.

54
00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:47.767
You have an LLM span in orange.

55
00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.869
You have your chain spans in blue.

56
00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:52.672
And then your tool spans in yellow.

57
00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:55.475
Traces and spans are captured
in your application through a process

58
00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:56.943
called instrumentation.

59
00:02:56.943 --> 00:03:00.747
Instrumentation refers
to the practice of marking which functions

60
00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:02.282
or code blocks within your code

61
00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:05.351
you want to track as spans
and what attributes

62
00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.687
you want to attach to those spans.

63
00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.356
You can do this
manually with clauses or decorators,

64
00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.559
as you'll see
in the next notebook as well.

65
00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:16.729
However, tools like Phoenix do automate
some of this process for you, especially

66
00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:21.367
if you're using popular libraries
like OpenAI or LlamaIndex or LangChain.

67
00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:25.171
Observability
is important for a few different reasons.

68
00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:28.174
First, when you're getting off the ground
and building your application,

69
00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:31.244
observability
really simplifies the debugging process.

70
00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.345
As you're building.

71
00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:35.915
It's much easier to debug
by going through a nice visual trace

72
00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:39.686
than it is to pore over print statements
and logs in your application,

73
00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.222
so it makes it easier to get going.

74
00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:44.624
But then, as you start
launching your application to multiple

75
00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:47.927
different users, you start moving towards
production or testing

76
00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:51.965
even. Then, it gives you a detailed log
of all of the calls made to

77
00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:54.968
your application and all of the different
inputs that you're receiving.

78
00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.637
So it gives you a large database
of information

79
00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:00.673
of all of your different application runs,
so you can monitor how it's performing.

80
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:06.246
And then these traces will become the
bedrock that is used to run evaluation.

81
00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:11.117
So later notebooks will have you export
data from Phoenix that you can then use

82
00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:15.021
for evaluations at scale across
multiple runs of your application.

83
00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:17.757
Finally, putting these together,

84
00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.326
observability helps
you really start to understand

85
00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:23.863
and eventually control some of the more
unpredictable behavior of LLMs.

86
00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:27.400
LLMs are by their very nature,
unpredictable in some cases,

87
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.768
and the best way to deal with

88
00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:32.772
that is by monitoring them and later
evaluating them within your application.

89
00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:36.909
In this lesson, you've learned
what observability is, how it works,

90
00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:38.378
and why it's important,

91
00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:41.614
as well as some of the basic building
blocks of observability like traces

92
00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.651
and spans, and how those traces in spans
are collected.

93
00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:48.921
In the next notebook, you'll implement
some of the instrumentation techniques

94
00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.723
that you saw in these slides,

95
00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:53.760
and you'll set up your first Phoenix
instance here to collect those traces.

